1. A spike script, scripts/spike_meta_upload.py, that I will run outside your sandbox (you have no network): upload one small PDF through the Files API (purpose "user_data"), send it to muse-spark-1.3 with a structured-output schema for spec tables, and upload one short H.264 MP4 for Spark video understanding (timestamped event list) and SAM video segmentation with a box prompt. It saves key-free raw responses as fixtures under backend/tests/fixtures/upload/. Until it runs, fixtures are PROVISIONAL and labelled as such. Use a public-domain or self-made test PDF (e.g. a generated one-page spec table) and a short self-made clip (ffmpeg from engine_bay_02.jpg is fine for shape-checking); no copyrighted service-manual pages in the repo.
2. PDF manual pages and scan-tool PDFs → Spark → SpecRange objects (schema in packages/schemas/, with page citation, content_hash of the source blob, extractor id) that become evidence/expected-value sources (test_result.expected.source.kind = manual_page). The LLM never sets probabilities; schema-validate everything.
3. CSV logs (Torque, Car Scanner, FORScan exports) parsed deterministically (no LLM) and mapped to PIDs/observations, with golden fixture files you write yourself.
4. Photo upload reusing the live keyframe path; video upload → Spark event list → sub-clips to SAM video (MP4/MOV H.264 only, min 50 billed frames, at most 3 tracked objects).
5. Endpoints added to the AGENTS.md API contract style (POST /sessions/{id}/uploads ...), Android upload entry point (system file picker, no new permissions beyond what's needed), and budget/caching through the existing MetaClient so REPLAY_USE_CACHED_LLM replays cost nothing.

